module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.by_ref_primitive_over_a_generic_call_argument_infers_from_the_borrow
import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
// A RUNTIME PRIMITIVE THAT BORROWS ITS ARGUMENT IS ALSO A TYPE-INFERENCE SITE, and the two fail apart.
// `v1_rt::sorted_map_keys` takes `&HashMap<K, V>`; over a generic call whose type arguments nothing else
// in the expression states, rustc invents them and they never resolve. The emitter has always had to
// know this, which is why the by-ref bridge roster exists -- but the roster answers WHO borrows, never
// WHAT the borrowed value's type arguments are, and a repair that stops at the roster leaves the call
// exactly as rustc found it.
data by_ref_primitive_over_a_generic_call_argument_infers_from_the_borrow: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "by_ref_primitive_over_a_generic_call_argument_infers_from_the_borrow" as NonEmptyStr,
  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "THE CONSUMING CHANGE IS NAMED: the corpus change that introduces the first eligible by-ref-over-generic call site ships the annotation arm, its emit-info permission and its executing claim together -- the machinery is recorded, not remembered, at branch xfer/crisp-bee-15509-annotation-arm (1bcee350bc10), held out of #15509 by review 91465 under DESIGN 5 (an unexecuted arm ships with its first consumer or not at all). two capabilities, named separately because they close different gaps. (1) GENERALITY: a second call shape needs the callee's type arguments named -- `rust_call_type_argument_witnesses` takes the application plan and nothing else, so widening from the by-ref bridge population to every declared call is a new call site in `emit_typed_call`, not a fork of the annotator. The one shape that cannot ride that widening as things stand is a METHOD callee needing its own type arguments: `ExprMethodCall` is deliberately outside `rust_by_ref_bridge_expression_is_call`, because the only renderer that spends the permission is `emit_typed_call`'s declared-call path and the method-call renderers write no clause -- granting there would put a flag no renderer consumes onto the method call's own sub-expression renders. That shape needs a spender in the method-call renderer first, which is that renderer's change to make, not a second annotator. (2) EXECUTION: a sanctioned way for a claim to invoke the target compiler. Until that route exists, an arm over emitted text can be pinned but not run, which is why this row cannot yet claim the rung above: the rule's own execution today is a hand-run rustc differential, labelled as such, not a lane.",
    },
  },
  receipts: [
    "summary: a by-ref runtime primitive is emitted over a call argument whose type arguments nothing states, and rustc is left to invent them: the primitive's parameter is the only evidence, so the call is refused or, worse, resolved to arguments the program never wrote.",
    "invalid_state: the emitter writes `v1_rt::sorted_map_keys(&<generic call>)` -- the borrow, and nothing else, is what should say what `K` and `V` are; the checker has accepted the program, so no diagnostic stands between it and a rustc refusal.",
    "harm: the seed does not build. This is not a warning that can be deferred: the generated mirror is the seed, so the failure is on the self-hosting path, in the mirror's own text, with no source line of a person's to point at.",
    "distinguishing_facts: the checker accepts the program and rustc refuses it, so the defect is visible only at the emission target; the argument is a CALL, not a name (a name already states its carrier, which is why the plain borrow stays correct there); and the roster predicate the emitter does consult -- name membership in the by-ref bridge registry -- cannot distinguish the two cases, because both are the same primitive.",
    "a by-ref primitive over a call argument with nothing to infer from** (a runtime primitive whose parameter is a reference is emitted over a call whose type arguments no other part of the expression states, so the borrow is the only evidence rustc has and the call resolves to arguments the program never wrote, or is refused.",
    "OCCURRENCE 2026-10-29, PR #15164, witnesses run 40968160998 at head 47612d0cb20d (branch `map-keys-home`, runner srv4-09-1793313729-1310342, created 2026-10-29T22:44:41Z, conclusion failure): the emitted crate was refused -- `V2-NATIVE REFUSAL cause=EmittedCompilerBuildFailed first_error=error[E0308]: mismatched types @ src/v1/v2-emitted-policy-unit-6/src/../../stage0/src/v2_compiler_occurrence_resolution_census.rs:428:153`, status 101, `diagnostic=unattributed`. The retained log keeps the refusal line and not the rustc body, so the tie to this row is the source at that head: `src/v2/compiler/occurrence_resolution_census.dag:440` reads `let conflicting = map_keys(m: fold_list(xs: occurrence_resolution_duplicates(entries: entries), empty: empty_map(), cons: fn(m, id) { map_insert(m: m, key: id, value: true) }))` -- a by-ref bridge (`map_keys`) emitted over a generic call (`fold_list`) whose accumulator the borrow alone was meant to pin. Same root as the 2026-11-01 `std.types` occurrence below it, three days earlier, and the reason the rule is written for every by-ref primitive call rather than for the one site that happened to fail first.",
    "OCCURRENCE 2026-11-01, PR #15509, heal run 41299024017: the second `rebuild_from_installed` of the seed refused the regenerated `std.types` mirror -- `error[E0308]: mismatched types --> src/v1/stage0/src/std_types.rs:168:9 __by_ref_arg … expected im::HashMap<_, _>, found Rc<im::HashMap<_, bool>>`. Two things in that one message are the class. `im::HashMap<_, _>` is an annotation whose type arguments were never resolved: the rule that writes it had consulted the checker and the checker had not finished. `Rc<im::HashMap<_, bool>>` is the carrier the emitted expression actually has, which is not the spelling the checker's renderer writes for a modelled `Map<String, Bool>`. The first repair attempt bound the VALUE at the modelled spelling and destroyed the deref coercion the plain borrow had been relying on; the second bound it as a REFERENCE inside a block, which the borrow checker refused outright (the temporary dies at the block's brace, before the call that uses the reference) -- both accounts are the `E0716` shape in the differential below. What the rule does instead leaves the borrow exactly as it was and makes the CALL name the arguments rustc had been left to invent.",
    "THE DECISION IS READ FROM THE PLAN AND FROM THE TREE, NEVER FROM THE RENDERING. The witness for a callee's type parameter is the pair the call's own application plan already carries: `substitution_basis`, the declaration-side type with its generic leaves stamped `TypeVariable { id: <binder> }` by `v1.compiler.lookup` `declaration_substitution_basis`, and `declared_type`, the site-side type with the callee's substitution already applied by `v1.compiler.infer` `build_call_application_plan`. Laid position against position they state the instantiation the emitter was missing; neither side states it alone. This is the fact `rust_witness_carrier_from_witness_headed_type_node`'s next-rung trigger records as absent -- \"no per-call type-argument substitution is recorded\" -- and what is absent is the CHANNEL that comment reads, `emit_info.expected_type`, which carries the enclosing expression's type rather than this argument's. Whether a witness may be written is decided by the seed's OWN resolution authority, `v1.compiler.infer_types` `is_fully_resolved` -- the walk the checker asks before it commits a type, thirteen times in `v1.compiler.infer` alone -- asked with the same `source_indices` the emitter already carries. Its `type_resolution_verdict` refuses a `TypeVariable` below an outer `Resolved`, an `Arrow` whose parameter types are unresolved, a container under its declared arity or with no spellable container, and a divergent expression, and its dominance order reports the strongest reason it found. NOT by a local walk, and NOT by testing rendered text for `<_,` or `, _>`: the rendered-text test was this row's first revision and review 89681's finding (a weaker second reading of a fact the checker already states, blind to `(_, i64)`, `Fn(_) -> i64` and `Vec<(i64, _)>`, quietly wrong whenever the renderer changes its spacing), and the local walk was this row's second -- review 90858 found a `rust_type_node_is_fully_resolved` this PR had minted beside `is_fully_resolved` with no stated reason under a comment that called it existing code, and reading the verdict showed that walk was also weaker (it passed an `Arrow` without asking its params, and a container without its arity or spelling), so it is deleted rather than documented. If a witness shape ever needs a judgement the verdict lacks, the repair is at `is_fully_resolved`, never in a second check beside it; the one known gap it carries is the reason `v1.compiler.infer` `fold_accumulator_is_unapplied_generic` exists -- an unapplied generic accumulator expansion, a `Disj` or `Conj` still carrying its declared params, reads as fully resolved to `is_fully_resolved` -- and it is named as a limitation where this rule asks. \"In a closed system a heuristic is never necessary: the richer source always exists or can be written.\" (DESIGN section 4.)",
    "THE REFUSAL IS LOUD AND LOCATED, AND `::<_>` IS NEVER EMITTED. Three states produce no annotation: the callee's declaration cannot be reached at the site; a declared parameter has no witness, or witnesses that render differently; a witness is not fully resolved. Each emits `compile_error!` naming the callee and the parameter, through `emit_rust_compile_error_expr` -- the same idiom this file uses to refuse an unestablished resource binding -- so the generated artifact carries the reason at the site instead of degrading into the shape that was refused. A `::<_>` would hand rustc the very guess this rule was written to remove, which would make the repair a silent widen of the defect, so the option is not in the code.",
    "WHAT IS EXECUTED, AND WHAT IS NOT. EXECUTED, by hand, with commands, compiler version (rustc 1.99.1) and full output reproduced in the PR body: a five-shape differential over programs with the same inference structure as the emitted one -- main's plain borrow compiles; the binding-block form this row's first repair emitted is refused `error[E0716]: temporary value dropped while borrowed`, which is the finding that ended that revision; the annotated call compiles, runs and asserts its result; the refusal form prints its own located message; and the branch-call form -- `sorted_map_keys(&if flag { fold_list(..) } else { rc_empty_map() })`, the shape the grant must never reach -- compiles and asserts its result with NO annotation, so the branches' own context is what pins those callees and a clause there would be unwanted, with an unreadable witness turning it into a `compile_error!` at a call that never had the defect. That is a hand run, labelled as one, not a lane. NOT EXECUTED: the three arms in `test.claim.by_ref_bridge_argument_declared_carrier_witness` pin EMITTED TEXT, so they are shape evidence -- red when the rule is deleted (the required substring disappears and the plain form returns), red when it is widened (the name arm forbids the wrapper returning where no clause is needed, and the branch arm forbids a clause on a call that is not the borrowed expression) -- and they do NOT establish that what they pin compiles. No lane under `.github/workflows` evaluates the claim pool those arms belong to. Saying that plainly is the obligation, not a hedge: DESIGN section 5 wants a consumer green by execution, and the route that would give this rule one is the second capability in `trigger_capability`.",
    "POPULATION, MEASURED, WITH THE COMMAND, AND WHAT STAYS UNKNOWN. At head 4dc4d091569c (main d2d681a68267, 2026-11-04), with the by-ref roster taken from the `passes_by_ref: true` rows of `dag/extdeps/languages/rust/emit.dag` (38 names): `grep -rEc --include=*.rs '(<roster>)\\(&' src/v1/stage0/src/` counts 1,122 plain by-ref sites; `grep -rEo --include=*.rs '(<roster>)\\(\\s*(?:Rc::new\\()?&([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9:]*)\\s*\\(' src/v1/stage0/src/` counts 62 sites whose bridged argument is a CALL, over 38 distinct (bridge, callee) pairs; and `grep -rEh --include=*.rs '^(pub )?fn <callee>\\b' src/v1/stage0/src/` for each distinct callee finds NONE declared with type parameters. So today all 62 take the arm that keeps the plain borrow, the emission is byte-identical to main's over the population that was counted, and no committed mirror exercises the annotation. The count is of a borrow whose argument IS a call, and it is not a count of every borrow: a borrow of an `if`, `match`, field access or block takes no arm at all, because the permission is granted by `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `rust_by_ref_bridge_grant` only when the borrowed expression is itself a call -- a clause names a callee and only a call has one -- so the calls such a borrow renders inside it are emitted exactly as on main and can never be refused for a defect they do not have. That shape is the third program in the witness file, not a measured population. UNKNOWN and not claimed: the emitted v2 closure is produced during a lane and never committed, so no revision can be grepped for it. Its evidence is indirect -- the v2-bootstrap lane at this head compiles that closure against main's plain-borrow emission and passes, and a generic callee behind a by-ref borrow there is exactly what refused run 40968160998 -- so the honest statement is that no known site exercises the arm, not that none can exist.",
    "CEILING, NOT REACHED. The rule names the callee's type arguments wherever the plan pins them and refuses at the site wherever it does not. What holds it at `Mitigatable` is not the rule's shape but its standing: no lane runs its claims, no committed site reaches its annotation, and its scope is the by-ref bridge population rather than every declared call. The two capabilities above are what would close it; neither is built here, and a row that claimed the rung anyway would be citing as coverage exactly the thing it cannot yet run.",
  ],
  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.emit_rust", decl_name: "rust_by_ref_bridge_argument", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_types", decl_name: "is_fully_resolved", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.by_ref_bridge_argument_declared_carrier_witness", decl_name: "by_ref_bridge_over_a_name_keeps_the_plain_borrow", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
